{:edb/db-before-t 1293 :edb/db-after-t 1294 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534606 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:19:01.343Z" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092064 1100 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}=\\dfrac{x}{3}+\\frac{x^2}{3^2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{x^3}{3^3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{x^4}{3^4\\cdot 2}+\\cdots \\,.\n$$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092064 1100 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}=\\dfrac{x}{3}+\\frac{x^2}{3^2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{x^3}{3^3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{x^4}{3^4\\cdot 2}+\\cdots \\,$." 13194139534606 false] [17592186092064 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define\n\n$$\na_n= \\dfrac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{x^{n + 1}}{3^{n + 1}\\sqrt{n + 1}} \\cdot \\frac{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}{x^{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{\\sqrt{n}x}{3\\sqrt{n + 1}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\mid x\\begin{vmatrix}}{3}lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n + 1}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid x \\mid}{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid x\\end{vmatrix}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L < 1$. In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mid x \\mid}{3}< 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n$\\mid x \\mid < 3$.\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $3$, and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-3,3)$.\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1$. So, when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=3$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=3$, we have\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{x^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{3^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}; = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} + ⋯$,\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test. So the endpoint $x=3$ is *not* included in our interval.\n- When $x=-3$, we have\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{x^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-3)^{n})/(3^{n}\\sqrt{n}); = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{n}); =-1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} + ⋯$,\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-3$ *is* included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[MATH: [-3,3).]$" 13194139534606 false] [17592186092064 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define $a_n=  \\dfrac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{3}^{n+1}\\sqrt{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n}x}{3\\sqrt{n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L <    1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|x|}{3}<   1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n|x| < 3.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $3,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-3,3).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=3,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=3$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=3$ is *not* included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-3)^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-3$ *is* included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-3,3).$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092075 1100 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n}x^n}{n \\cdot 2^n} = -\\frac{x}{1 \\cdot 2} +  \\frac{x^2}{2 \\cdot 2^2}-\\frac{x^3}{3 \\cdot 2^3}+\\frac{x^4}{4 \\cdot 2^4}+\\cdots \\,.\n$$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092075 1100 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n}x^n}{n \\cdot 2^n} = -\\frac{x}{1 \\cdot 2} + \\frac{x^2}{2 \\cdot 2^2}-\\frac{x^3}{3 \\cdot 2^3}+\\frac{x^4}{4 \\cdot 2^4}+\\cdots \\,$." 13194139534606 false] [17592186092075 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{(-1)^{n}x^n}{n \\cdot 2^n}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-1)^{n + 1}x^{n + 1})/((n + 1) \\cdot 2^{n + 1}) \\cdot (n \\cdot 2^{n})/((-1)^{n}x^{n}) \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid (-nx)/(2(n + 1)) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{x}{2} & \\cdot lim_(n → ∞)\\frac{n}{n + 1} \\\\ = & \\frac{x}{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\mid x \\mid}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L< 1$. In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}x &}{2} < 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad & x\\end{vmatrix} < 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=2$." 13194139534606 false] [17592186092075 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define $a_n=\\dfrac{(-1)^{n}x^n}{n \\cdot 2^n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)\\cdot {2}^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{n\\cdot {2}^{n}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-nx}{2(n+1)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{x}{2}|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+1} \\\\  & =|\\frac{x}{2}|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<         1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|x|}{2}  < 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad |x| < 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=2.$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092076 1100 "Find the radius and interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{ (-1)^n x^{n}}{n!}.\n$$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092076 1100 "Find the radius and interval of convergence for the series\n$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{(-1)^n x^{n}}{n!}$." 13194139534606 false] [17592186092076 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{(-1)^n x^{n}}{n!}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-1)^{n + 1}x^{n + 1})/((n + 1)!) \\cdot (n!)/((-1)^{n}x^{n}) \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid - (x)/((n + 1)) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-x & lim_(n → ∞)\\frac{1}{n + 1} \\\\ = & x\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L< 1$. In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$. Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty$, and the interval of convergence is $x\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$." 13194139534606 false] [17592186092076 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define $a_n=\\dfrac{ (-1)^n x^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\,\\frac{x}{(n+1)}| \\\\  & =|-x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<       1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186092214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298457 13194139534606 true] [17592186092214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298461 13194139534606 true] [17592186092214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298471 13194139534606 true] [17592186092214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298478 13194139534606 true] [17592186092214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298492 13194139534606 true] [17592186092215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298408 13194139534606 true] [17592186092215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298436 13194139534606 true] [17592186092215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298443 13194139534606 true] [17592186092215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298450 13194139534606 true] [17592186092215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298485 13194139534606 true] [17592186092221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298415 13194139534606 true] [17592186092221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298422 13194139534606 true] [17592186092221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298429 13194139534606 true] [17592186092221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298464 13194139534606 true] [17592186092221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298499 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298409 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1089 "q-12406" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1100 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{(n!)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1113 #uuid "56b9b40b-4ff6-5e79-a12a-9356f4de2cd9" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298408 1145 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{x}^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2(n+1)+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =|{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298410 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298411 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298412 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298413 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298414 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1041 "selection" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298410 13194139534606 true] [17592186298409 1110 #uuid "1d969feb-b7e2-562a-85a2-76dbd6321957" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298410 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534606 true] [17592186298410 1111 #uuid "b7fcdcbb-7e40-5819-9546-36ab13ec6019" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298411 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534606 true] [17592186298411 1111 #uuid "78263d88-f7c5-5aa4-abcf-d51f831193c6" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298412 1034 "x\\in [-1,1]" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534606 true] [17592186298412 1111 #uuid "e8b606fb-b59a-5fed-85ae-705aad4dc343" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298413 1034 "x\\in (-1,\\infty )" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534606 true] [17592186298413 1111 #uuid "5f488fec-30c9-5a47-83fe-8bff9bd4ca55" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298414 1034 "x\\in (-1,1)" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534606 true] [17592186298414 1111 #uuid "4a70ad96-cb63-5898-8b72-bb5ba68d28ac" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298416 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1089 "q-125293" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1100 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{1/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\cdot {2}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3/2}\\cdot {3}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{4}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1113 #uuid "2ec06a56-d8fb-58c5-a97d-15ca74e81e31" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298415 1145 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)/2}(n+1)^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x{n}^{4}}{{2}^{1/2}(n+1)^{4}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\sqrt{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\sqrt{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\sqrt{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\sqrt{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{n/2}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}=1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-\\sqrt{2})^{n}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{2}^{n/2}}{{2}^{n/2}{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{4}}=-1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}].$" 13194139534606 true] [17592186298416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298417 13194139534606 true] [17592186298416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298418 13194139534606 true] [17592186298416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298419 131941